\details{
The Wald distribution describes the time it takes for a Wiener diffusion
process starting at 0 to reach a threshold \code{bs} > 0, given a positive drift
rate \code{drift} > 0. That time is then shifted by a non-decision time \code{ndt}.

It is mathematically equivalent to shifting an Inverse Gaussian distribution
with mean \code{bs / drift} and shape \code{bs^2}. In the \code{brms} family the drift rate
is named \code{mu}, since \code{brms} requires a parameter of that name.

\code{ndt}, \code{poutlier} and \code{minrt} mean exactly what they do in \code{\link[=rt_lognormal]{rt_lognormal()}},
and \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=without_outliers]{without_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=p_outlier]{p_outlier()}} and
\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} all work here too. See \code{?rrt_lognormal} for why \code{ndt} is
expressed directly in seconds rather than as a fraction of the fastest
observed response, what the half Student-t outlier component is for, and why
\code{minrt} is a constant on the family rather than a \code{dpar}.

The Wald density vanishes at the shift with all derivatives, like the
LogNormal, so \code{ndt} is well behaved here and there is no unbounded-likelihood
boundary of the kind \code{\link[=rt_gamma]{rt_gamma()}} and \code{\link[=rt_weibull]{rt_weibull()}} have at shape \verb{< 1}.

The random generation algorithm is that of Michael, Schucany, and Haas
(1976), as used in the \code{statmod} package.
}
